.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt FUGUSEED-WORDS 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fuguseed-words
.Nd build the printed word sheet, and check a built one
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Cm build
.Ar list
.Op Fl -date Ar YYYY-MM-DD
.Nm
.Cm check
.Ar sheet
.Ar list
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
builds the printed word sheet of a word list, and it proves a built
sheet against that list.
The sheet is the address space of three dice: one throw selects one
word of the list, and the person does no arithmetic.
.Pp
.Nm
sees no seed word.
It reads no word of a person and it prints none, so it can run on any
computer.
The offline procedure that uses the sheet lives in
.Xr fuguseed 7 .
.Pp
Both verbs take a word list file.
.Nm
computes the SHA-256 of that file, and it refuses every list but the
English word list of BIP39.
.Nm
.Fl -help
names the path of the shipped list.
.Ss The build verb
.Nm
.Cm build
.Ar list
writes the sheet as HTML to standard output.
The sheet is one standalone file: the build inlines the style sheet.
.Pp
The option
.Fl -date Ar YYYY-MM-DD
sets the build date of the two footers.
Two builds of one list on one date give one file, byte for byte.
Without the option, the build takes the date of the day, in UTC.
.Ss The check verb
.Nm
.Cm check
.Ar sheet
.Ar list
proves that
.Ar sheet
is the sheet of
.Ar list .
The verb reads the whole sheet as one strict grammar, from the first
byte to the last.
It compares the inlined style sheet with the shipped one byte for
byte, and it compares each caption, each header, and each of the 2048
cells with the text of its position.
.Pp
The verb is silent on success.
On a failure it prints one line for each defect on standard error.
A line names the offset of the first unexpected byte, or the block,
the row, and the column of a cell.
.Ss The sheet
The sheet holds two sides for A4 paper.
Side 1 holds the YELLOW blocks 1 to 4, and side 2 holds the YELLOW
blocks 5 to 8.
Each block is one table of 16 BLUE rows and 16 RED columns, so the
eight blocks hold the 2048 words of the list.
.Pp
Each side holds a legend that names the three dice, and a footer that
names the side, the standard, the SHA-256 of the list, and the build
date.
.Ss The print
Print the sheet on A4 paper, on both sides of one sheet, at 100
percent scale, and in color.
The color of a label is a second name of the die: the captions are
YELLOW, the column headers are RED, and the row headers are BLUE.
A black print keeps the names, because each label holds the name of
the color as a word.
.Pp
Check the print against the file before you use it.
Each table must hold 16 rows and 16 columns, and no word may run over
two lines.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
.Nm
wrote the sheet, or the check found no defect.
.It 1
.Nm
could not read a file, the list had another SHA-256, or the check
found a defect.
.Nm
printed one line for each reason on standard error.
.It 2
The command line held no verb, a wrong argument count, or a malformed
option value.
.Nm
printed the usage line on standard error.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Build the sheet of the shipped word list:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguseed-words build english.txt > sheet.html
.Ed
.Pp
Build the sheet of one date, twice, and compare the two files:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguseed-words build --date 2026-01-01 english.txt > a.html
$ fuguseed-words build --date 2026-01-01 english.txt > b.html
$ cmp a.html b.html
.Ed
.Pp
Check a sheet before you print it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ fuguseed-words check sheet.html english.txt
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr fuguseed-qr 1 ,
.Xr fuguseed 7
.Sh STANDARDS
The word list comes from BIP39, the mnemonic code for the generation
of a deterministic key.
The sheet is HTML5.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
The check proves the sheet, not the print.
A printer can drop a color, cut a margin, or scale a page, and no
program sees that.
Compare the print with the file.
